[Music]  this is apple's lightning charger cable  and it hasn't changed in 10 years but  the eu wants apple to change that and  adopt a more universal cable enter usbc  apple has been fighting it let's talk  about why that is and how the company's  going about it  usbc is a free open standard supported  by a network of more than 700 companies  it's even supported by apple then why is  apple the only company that hasn't added  it to its smartphones  the company switched from this 30-pin  connector to lightning in 2012. at the  time lightning was groundbreaking but as  the years went on a shift happened  lightning started to fade as most new  ipads and all of apple's new macs got  usbc ports now just a couple of apple  products still have lightning ports and  most importantly iphones apple's most  popular product  the eu argues that it's both expensive  and inconvenient for consumers to have  to carry multiple charges for multiple  devices and so now policymakers are  proposing a shift one that would require  iphone sold in europe to have a usbc  port that legislation if passed this  year would give companies two years to  make the change for apple to meet that  deadline the company would already need  to be working on a usbc iphone or figure  out another way out now there is a  precedent for this situation in 2009  tech companies and the eu reached a  voluntary agreement and because of that  micro usbs became a lot more common in  2009 we had more than 30  3-0 charging solutions currently we have  only three main  possibilities in 2019 the eu said that  removing all of these exclusive chargers  would save around 13 000 tons of  electronic waste per year electronic  waste is still one of the fastest  growing waste streams which makes this  type of base prevention particularly  important so why is apple fighting all  of this anyway analysts tell me it comes  down to three main things money control  and design apple makes money from all  the lightning tables it sells and it  also makes money on non-apple products  made by third parties that go through  its made for iphone program  and then there's control without the  lightning cable apple doesn't have as  much control over its ecosystem apple  learned the hard way with the 30 pin  when they discontinued that that ended  up causing some angst with the apple  users they moved too quickly away from  that so apple wants to not uh go too  fast and lastly experts tell me that the  lightning port allows apple to make  slimmer phones with a more compact  opening it's smaller which gives them  more flexibility to design other things  into the iphone and even though it may  seem like not a big piece of it uh every  little piece counts when it comes to  miniaturizing what's in the phone apple  said in a statement we remain concerned  that strict regulation mandating just  one type of connector stifles innovation  rather than encouraging it which in turn  will harm consumers in europe and around  the world but what happens if the eu  actually succeeds clearly switching an  iphone from one charging standard to  another isn't impossible this college  student studying robotics even did it  this is it people the world's first usbc  iphone  but watch this  apple has another option for getting  around usbc every single iphone since  the iphone 8 supports qi wireless  charging if apple created iphones that  didn't have a single port but supported  wireless charging the company would get  around the eu's plans but today there's  an engineering problem you're getting  speed charges that are five ten times  longer when you compare to the faster  charging uh cables today  if the eu has its way apple might have  to create a specialized iphone just for  europe or change all of its smartphones  globally but frankly i'd be happy to not  have to carry around so many cables to  charge my devices or have to try to  figure out which end of the cable goes  where if you want to find out more about  what apple or other smartphone makers  are working on subscribe to my channel  and let me know in the comments section  what you think i should look into next  you

